{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And he came as far as Hebron.</b> It does not say 'and they came,' for it refers back to Caleb alone, who came to the Cave of Machpelah in Kiryat Arba, which is Hebron, and prostrated himself on the graves of the fathers, as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: that he came to pray there, to be saved from the counsel of the spies. For Joshua was already saved from this when he received his blessing from Moses, who said to him: ״May YHWH save you from the counsel of the spies.״" "<b>And I saw</b> in the Midrash: ״And he came as far as Hebron״ — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, who came to Hebron to inform the fathers that the time had arrived for Israel to enter the land, because of the oath He had sworn to them." "<b>And Hebron was built seven years before Zoan of Egypt.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this comes to inform us that it is more ancient than the kingdom of Zoan." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: Hebron was built as one out of seven in Zoan. The explanation of this is that Hebron was the refuse of the land of Israel, and therefore it was set aside for a burial ground, and it was built as one out of seven in Zoan, which is the principal seat of the kingdom of Egypt, as it is said (Isaiah 30:4): ״כי היו בצען שריו״, and it is written of Egypt (Genesis 13:10): ״כגן ה׳ כארץ מצרים״. And Scripture comes to inform us thereby of the praise of the land of Israel."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:04.814439545Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}